#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:5
#: b51109915df7478189ac13956e590e61
msgid "Radio Control Calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:7
#: fa38fdbd164244a5ac9152e04b2164af
msgid "This article shows how to perform radio control calibration using Mission Planner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:10
#: d1fc5ec6daaf4cc9a16bb9da51e424c2
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:12
#: 0e61939333e341f4b23da6f21857e6a8
msgid "RC transmitters allow the pilot to set the flight mode, control the vehicle's movement and orientation and also turn on/off auxiliary functions (i.e. raising and lowering landing gear, etc)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:14
#: 7fa56e6f19754c1eaf68612cd1a14164
msgid "RC Calibration involves capturing each RC input channel's minimum, maximum and \"trim\" values so that ArduPilot can correctly interpret the input."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:17
#: 72379e661a214c0092aa3cd1ee9fc14c
msgid "Check the Transmitter's Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:19
#: 67264830f3114c60b10134721f4d1f1e
msgid "Ensure the battery is disconnected (this is important because it is possible to accidentally arm the vehicle during the RC calibration process)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:20
#: d1c5e6ff8cbe414d9b20571ab1ff4f50
msgid "Ensure the RC receiver is connected to the autopilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:21
#: 42ba5d6e947942cfb58d074bfe9c54ff
msgid "Turn on your RC transmitter and if it has \"trim tabs\" ensure they are in the middle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:22
#: 60be1b2f5a73471fa48764a919b7814b
msgid "Connect the autopilot to the PC using a USB cable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:23
#: 87a540618b7d46a284aab912b2e7bc9f
msgid "On the Mission Planner press the \"Connect\" button and open Mission Planner's **INITIAL SETUP \\| Mandatory Hardware \\| Radio Calibration** screen"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:24
#: 7bd258eed489470abdbc87558c4be373
msgid "Some green bars should appear showing the ArduPilot is receiving input from the Transmitter/Receiver. If no bars appear check the receiver's LED:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:26
#: 7efe55a8deb84ecaa4696785d0f8cd43
msgid "No lights may indicate that it is incorrectly wired to the autopilot.  Look for connectors that may have been inserted upside down"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:27
#: fe1ca8dbb1554bd9a56c9e286ef948ab
msgid "A Red or flashing LED may indicate that your RC transmitter/receiver need be bound.  See the manual that came with your RC equipment for instructions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:32
#: 67d6775bb3984b1eb1d10f9c19a0344b
msgid "Check the channel mapping in the transmitter (ie. check which inputs channels are controlled by the transmitter's sticks, switches and knobs) by moving the sticks, knobs and switches and observing which (if any) green bars move.  If this is the first time the transmitter is being used with ArduPilot it is likely that the transmitter's channel mapping will need to be changed and normally this is done on the transmitter itself using its built-in configuration menu"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:34
#: 4d37c56829bc49ecaf1292831640f372
msgid "Determine if your transmitter is Mode1 or Mode2 (see below)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:35
#: 0deece98f21e4da8a40cdebf07b2184d
msgid "Roll stick should control channel 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:36
#: 87e79ac00a984874bde323dc0a279952
msgid "Pitch stick should control channel 2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:37
#: d7b2095fbac8418ba702c5689d503c9c
msgid "Throttle stick should control channel 3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:38
#: 5a21ea0bf4194d3a953e06eebc6b88a4
msgid "Yaw stick should control channel 4"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:39
#: 24c0e61fff3c496f8813edfbe7ade409
msgid "A 3 or 6 position switch (to control the flight mode) should be setup to control Channel 5 (default,if using Copter) or Channel 8 (default, if using Rover or Plane).  This channel can be moved by setting the :ref:`FLTMODE_CH <FLTMODE_CH>` parameter in Plane or Copter, or :ref:`MODE_CH<MODE_CH>` in Rover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:40
#: 9daea2f7de254615a0859d0896e3dcb8
msgid "On Copter, a tuning knob should control Channel 6"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:41
#: 90b239637d8e470aa41a497536a49b34
msgid "On Copter and Rover, any remaining two or three position switches can be setup to control auxiliary functions by mapping them to channels 7 to 12"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:42
#: 193b0187dc1e4f588f73b72395955695
msgid "Note that for reversing motor controls on Plane and Rover, the throttle range is range is normally divided into two parts: - Reverse throttle control: From ``RCx_TRIM`` (typically 1500us) to ``RCx_MIN``, where x is usually \"3\" for normal RC throttle input channel. This corresponds to motor outputs from ``SERVOx_TRIM`` to ``SERVOx_MIN``, where \"x\" is the output controlling the motor, and assumes the ``SERVOx_REVERSE`` bit is not set. - Forward throttle control: From ``RCx_TRIM`` (typically 1500us) to ``RCx_MAX``, where x is usually \"3\" for normal RC throttle input channel. This corresponds to motor outputs from ``SERVOx_TRIM`` to ``SERVOx_MAX``, where \"x\" is the output controlling the motor, and assumes the ``SERVOx_REVERSE`` bit is not set. Using a spring-loaded stick may be most convenient, as it naturally returns to the neutral (stopped) position at ``RCx_TRIM``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:46
#: 3bce86cd77c3454ea0f90d42337db947
msgid "For Plane, it is possible to use an AUX RC switch to provide easie reverse thrust control using the entire throttle stick range on non-sprung throttle sticks. Also, many set up a switch controlled mix in their RC transmitter to map throttle low stick position to output ``RCx_TRIM`` after calibration and either output RC as the stick is raised for reverse or forward operation depending on the TX switch position, giving full throttle stick range to forward or reverse operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:48
#: fe61d377c76e4e3989a5ccf2bf3fec04
msgid "Move the transmitter's roll, pitch, throttle, and yaw sticks and ensure the green bars move in the correct direction:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:50
#: 5e533461e3644b689e934f7f1c324828
msgid "For roll, throttle and yaw channels, the green bars should move in the same direction as the transmitter's physical sticks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:51
#: 044403d87d814ab2baf5a11fa604d57d
msgid "For pitch, the green bar should move in the **opposite** direction to the transmitter's physical stick. **This is not the default for many transmitters.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:52
#: 02365ba78c22440e93b6044fa4a82166
msgid "If one of the green bars moves in the incorrect direction, reverse the channel in the transmitter itself so that logging and outputs etc. are consistent. You may instead reverse the channel in ArduPilot by checking the \"Reversed\" checkbox (if shown), or directly changing the ``RCx_REVERSED`` parameter (where \"x\" is the input channel from 1 to 4)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:58
#: 58fb04d6abec48b5bc3cdde5b7ab1454
msgid "Calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:60
#: a91da65d611049f794a2cb77206c1ee0
msgid "Open Mission Planner's **INITIAL SETUP \\| Mandatory Hardware \\| Radio Calibration** screen"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:61
#: 15fdf1af43d34ca288908f39ef14ad2b
msgid "Click on the green \"Calibrate Radio\" button on the bottom right"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:62
#: daabf2c773f248bebdab3f36f7fd1133
msgid "Press \"OK\" when prompted to check the radio control equipment is on, battery is not connected, and propellers are not attached"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:67
#: 0309ab8c1a634b9da43c8dba596d36c4
msgid "Move the transmitter's control sticks, knobs and switches to their limits.  Red lines will appear across the calibration bars to show minimum and maximum values seen so far"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:72
#: a4bc932e040f4bc994b695f06c8dfc26
msgid "Select **Click when Done**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:74
#: 41d83446e54b4577bc61c8c1ec9c88a7
msgid "A window will appear with the prompt, \"Ensure all your sticks are centered and throttle is down and click ok to continue\".  Move the throttle to zero and press \"OK\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:78
#: a536954e89e847b9ab4ffa8bb76b231c
msgid "Mission Planner will show a summary of the calibration data. Normal values are around 1100 for minimums and 1900 for maximums."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:83
#: f9f12c45016f44988d25805955ca1303
msgid "If when testing your throttle stick you notice that the motor turns in a single direction, check in the full parameters list that ``RCx_TRIM`` for the throttle channel is set to the motor controller's neutral value (usually about 1500 us). This should be detected and set automatically during calibration if you followed the procedure without forgetting to **center** your throttle in the last calibration step instead of the normal **low** position."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:86
#: 4b52e199ed7a49d9b377cc8ed7c542b7
msgid "Mode1 and Mode2 Transmitters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:88
#: b4c44a90df9b413b8476d4db8d8f8a85
msgid "There are two main transmitter configurations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:90
#: a157d5bac8804ae29d05458389db8af4
msgid "*Mode 1*: left stick controls pitch and yaw, the right stick will control throttle and roll."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:92
#: cea1dc3976114ce08e90eb898bcf3c3e
msgid "*Mode 2*: left stick controls throttle and yaw; the right stick will control pitch and roll."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:98
#: a2afdceb739e4f059beee9b55b3f8051
msgid "There are two alternative configurations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:100
#: 76468791017d4fe1b28a7692797286d9
msgid "*Mode 3*: left stick controls pitch and roll, the right stick will control throttle and yaw."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:102
#: a2f3a8fb4e5f46008d5b18f0a63d3ca5
msgid "*Mode 4*: left stick controls throttle and roll; the right stick will control pitch and yaw."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:105
#: 4d12dd324b9a4702ad468a3b7d3d021b
msgid "Mode 3 is the opposite of Mode 2 and Mode 4 is the opposite of Mode 1, giving complete right handed/left handed user options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-radio-control-calibration.rst:110
#: 7dffd823d84b493588431b95acda79b6
msgid "Channel mappings"
msgstr ""

